  "proposal": {
    "hypothesis": "The first challenger ends the city phase standing on the Peristyle crossing with full equipment, one bonus Prestige, and the officials faction at that same cell, while rivals are scattered mid-board; that positional carryover is a main reason the first challenger wins 37.5% of games (baseline holdout) instead of a fair quarter. Returning every pawn to its own gate at the phase transition makes succession a fresh race decided by equipment already collected, so late collectors keep a credible path and gate seats matter through route length rather than through one player's leftover head start.",
    "prediction": "First-challenger win share falls from about 0.375 to at most 0.32 on both suites; comeback wins (first challenger loses) rise correspondingly; median rounds rise by at most two (gate-to-faction walking); cancellations, draws, contest availability, and the four winning profiles stay near baseline.",
    "refutation_evidence": "First-challenger win share does not fall below its baseline value on both suites; or median rounds rise beyond the frozen guard; or the east gate (12 steps from the crossing) win share falls further below its baseline, showing the reset amplifies the known geometric disadvantage; or any mechanical or counterplay gate fails.",
    "rule_path": "succession.reset_positions",
    "value": true
  },
